A||||11||Tom88gt||pulsating brakes||||8||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||11-15-2001||07:29 PM||||My brakes are pulsating again.  The front rotors and pads are only a year old and the rotors appear to be true.  New lower ball joints a couple of months ago.  All the suspension stuff seems good and tight.  Any ideas?  ||||reg||8
Z||000001||shark93726||11-15-2001||08:03 PM||||Pulsating brakes usually results from the rotors having gotten overheated at one time resulting in "hard" and "soft" areas on the rotor, which means they wear unevenly, and after enough wear, the "hard" area is thicker, and "grabs" the caliper as it goes by each revolution.<P>You may have to get new rotors to get rid of the problem, if that is what it is.<P>It could be from a poor job of turning the rotors too, though.<P>Gerald Storvik||||reg||1
Z||000002||reddog||11-15-2001||08:07 PM||||Hey you must have ABS!!!!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> (JOKING)really may just be that the rotors need turned or they could be warped. If not you may have a problem in your hydraulics.Take it to a brake specialist!! <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||87fastback||11-15-2001||08:19 PM||||It is definately warped rotors.  Could have happened after some spirited driving then clamping on the brakes and holding them.  I have seen this countless times at the dealer.  Plus make sure you are using a "quality" replacement rotor as alot of the cheap rotors are very low quality and warp easily.  Do yourself a favor and buy GM parts.  You can remedy the problem by turning, but it will come back for sure!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||11-15-2001||08:19 PM||||I'll third the view for out of round rotors.  If they were turned last year they make have them ground down to the minimum, then with heavy braking, overheated and warped.  Rotor time$$$||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||11-15-2001||11:29 PM||||Well...the rotors were brand new last November.  I got them from the Fiero Store.  They were made in Taiwan but so are the rotors that AUto ZOne sells.  <P>Anyway, the Fiero Store sent me a replacement pair since they had a year warranty.  I got ready to swap them out today and when I got the wheels off and inspected the rotors closely, I couldn't detect any warpage.  So I'm really confussed, cause a pulsating brake pedal is supposed to always mean warped rotors.<P>I'm hesitant to swap out the rotors.  When I spin my old ones I see no wobble or anything unusual.||||reg||5
Z||000006||ITB 2M4||11-15-2001||11:49 PM||||Rotors that have enough run-out for you to see with your eyes would be totally unusable.  You have to remember that the feeling of the warped rotor is multiplied through the braking system back to your foot.  If you use the brakes hard, then don't give them a chance to cool off, sometimes they get a glazed spot to them.  But that would feel like they grab, then glide, grab, then glide.  You feel that when the car is almost ready to stop.  You won't feel that so much at higher speeds.  A rotor with excessive run-out will actually make the brake pedal 'pulse' under your foot.  Turning the rotor in either of those situations will cure the problem, if the rotor still has enough thickness to be cut.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||11-16-2001||12:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tom88gt:<BR><B>Well...the rotors were brand new last November.  I got them from the Fiero Store.  They were made in Taiwan but so are the rotors that AUto ZOne sells.  </B><P>This is the very reason why I shudder when everyone looks for the cheapest brake parts possible.  In the long run nothing beats good quality......but AC Delco rotors and you won't have any problems (or anything other than cheap Taiwan crap).  I have owned/driven many '88 Fieros and haven't had any real problems with warpage.....especially on near new rotors.  That just shouldn't happen.<P><B>I'm hesitant to swap out the rotors.  When I spin my old ones I see no wobble or anything unusual.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You won't necessarily see the warpage.....the best bet is to remove them and have a brake shop inspect them.  Once they get it on the lathe any warpage will become obvious.<P><BR>Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rodmcneill||11-16-2001||12:39 AM||||Maybe stating the obvious but you do have rotors on the rear also.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||11-16-2001||08:03 PM||||Most of the time, the only way you will see a warped rotor is with the proper tools.<P>The most critical spec for a rotor is "Max variation from parallel" which for ALL Fiero is 0.0005 inch. that's the one that will really get the pedal pumping....<P>the next is "Lateral Run out" that's .004 (84-87) and .003 (88) This one won't always make the pedal jump but it will wear the brake hardware and usually cause noise.<P>Another critical mistake made by people cutting brakes.... NEVER EVER cut a rotor to the minimum thickness stamped on the rotor. That is the DISCARD thickness. Any rotor that is worn to or below that number is to be scrapped. This includes groove wear!<P>The machining(turning) limit of most rotors is 8-10 thousanths (inch) above the discard thickness.<P>Brake rotor specifications can be found online in the parts database of PartAmerica.com <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000010||avengador1||11-16-2001||09:09 PM||||If you want to check the rotors for warpage you need a magnetic goose neck dial indicator that measures to the .0005" that is 1/2 of one thousand of an inch. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||11-17-2001||08:51 PM||||It depends on how the rotor is warped...<P>the lateral runout can be within spec but you can still fail parallelism. <P>A runout problem is like a bent rim, the wheel or rotor wobbles. As the rotor wobbles it works the caliper back and forth in the hardware. This doesn't always cause pedal pulsing. You may feal it thru the car body or steering wheel.<P>Paralellism is like you took the beads of a rim and pinched them in some on one side of the wheel. The rotor thickness actually changes in one or more areas. This usually the problem that pulses the pedal.<P>A dial gauge and mount are mainly to measure runout. You can't easily measure rotor thickness with it. <P>You need a deep reach micrometer to measure parallelism. You can do it with a regular micrometer but you may not be able to reach far enough onto allot of rotors.<P>To measure for parallelism I meausre rotor thickness in at various places around the rotor. (Number of places depends on rotor size and working area. I'd use 4-6 readings for a Fiero.) If the diferance between the largest and smallest reading is more than .0005 for a Fiero then the rotor needs machine work or replacement.<P>To measure runout, you mount the dial gauge so you can see it and spin the rotor thru one revolution. The min and max readings need to be within .004 for 84-87 or .003 for 88.<P>The single worse thing you can do is park a car with the brakes over heated. Never apply a parking brake on really hot rotors. If you think the brakes are heavily heated then drive around slow for a couple miles and let them cool down. If you park with them hot the pads will make them cool unevenly. If you pull the hand brake you make that even worse plus you have pressure on the rotor(drum) as it cools. (Yes, this applies to drum brake cars too.)||||reg||1
